Problem Statement:
Given an array of integers nums and an integer target, find two distinct elements in the array whose sum is equal to target.
Return the indices of those two elements. 

Brute Force Approach
Idea
The simplest approach is to examine every possible pair of elements.
We use two loops:
The first loop selects an element.
The second loop checks that element against every element that comes after it.
If their sum equals the target, return their indices.
If no pair is found, return an empty result.

Code:
vector<int> twoSum(vector<int>& nums, int target)
{
    int n = nums.size();

    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        for(int j = i + 1; j < n; j++)
        {
            if(nums[i] + nums[j] == target)
            {
                return {i, j};
            }
        }
    }

    return {};
}

Time Complexity: O(n²)
For every element, we may have to compare it with many other elements.

Space Complexity: O(1)
We are not using any extra data structure that grows with the input.
Although the solution is simple, it performs repeated comparisons.
For a large array, the number of pairs becomes very large.


Better Approach — Hashing
Idea
Instead of checking every pair, we can use a hash map to remember elements that we have already encountered.
For the current element:
current = nums[i]
we calculate the value that would be required to reach the target:
required = target - current
Then we check whether required has already appeared.If it has, we have found our pair.

Code:
vector<int> twoSum(vector<int>& nums, int target)
{
    unordered_map<int, int> mp;

    for(int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++)
    {
        int required = target - nums[i];

        if(mp.find(required) != mp.end())
        {
            return {mp[required], i};
        }

        mp[nums[i]] = i;
    }

    return {};
}

Time Complexity: O(n) average
We traverse the array once, and hash-map lookup is O(1) on average.

Space Complexity: O(n)
In the worst case, we may store almost every element in the hash map.
The brute-force approach searches for the matching element by scanning the array.
Hashing replaces that repeated scanning with a fast lookup.
This reduces the average time from:
O(n²) → O(n)


Optimal Approach — Hashing
For the standard Two Sum problem on an unsorted array, the hashing approach is already the optimal time-complexity solution.
There is no separate algorithm that improves the general case from O(n) to something smaller, because we need to inspect the input to determine whether the required pair exists.
Therefore, the optimal solution is the same one-pass hash-map approach.
Idea
Process the array from left to right.
For each element:
1. Calculate its complement.
2. Check whether the complement has already been seen.
3. If yes → return the two indices.
4. If no → store the current element and its index.

Code:
vector<int> twoSum(vector<int>& nums, int target)
{
    unordered_map<int, int> seen;

    for(int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++)
    {
        int complement = target - nums[i];

        if(seen.count(complement))
        {
            return {seen[complement], i};
        }

        seen[nums[i]] = i;
    }

    return {};
}

Time Complexity: O(n) average
We make one pass through the array and perform constant-time average hash-map operations.

Space Complexity: O(n)
The hash map can contain up to n elements.
For an unsorted array, O(n) is the best general time complexity because the algorithm may need to inspect every element.
